{
  "image_name": "2f6f6380886afcb07f68ff2171406ed0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs) sit at the center of a tumor-supporting communication network. In the middle, CAFs exchange signals with many immune and stromal cell types and reshape the extracellular matrix (ECM). Arrows from CAFs toward immune cells indicate mainly suppressive or recruiting effects: they appear to attract or polarize TH2 cells, Treg cells, and M2 tumor-associated macrophages through chemokines and cytokines such as CCL2/5/7, IL-1, IL-6, IL-13, IL-26, SDF1, and M-CSF. They also inhibit antitumor cells, including CD8+ T cells, NK cells, and dendritic cells, through mediators like TGFβ, PGE2, IDO, and VEGF.\n\nOn the stromal side, CAFs release matrix-remodeling enzymes (MMP1, MMP2, MMP3, MMP9) that act on the ECM, and they promote lymphatic endothelial cells via VEGFA and VEGFC. Reciprocal signaling with cancer cells is also emphasized: factors including IL-1β, SDF1, PDGF-B, HB-EGF, PDGF-D, FGF, and TGFβ1 connect CAFs to carcinoma cells. ECM components such as tenascin, periostin, and osteopontin feed into tumor-cell signaling, with YAP/TAZ, NOTCH3, and Hh shown as downstream pathways. Overall, the image depicts CAFs as organizers of immune evasion, matrix remodeling, and tumor progression.",
